# The layers a double count runs between, which is the evidence for an elimination. One row per
# `asrt:between` on a filed elimination: which party's filing, which layer of it, under which
# regime, and which version the composer read.
#
# The subject of an elimination
#   An elimination without this is a number with no subject. `eliminations/filed.sqlc` says a
#   composer removed 40 to 150 GPU-hours, most likely 90, from `merge-holding-composition/compute`.
#   It cannot say what was counted twice. These rows can: `compute-us` and `compute-pt`, both from
#   `group-parent`, both under the `group` regime. That is the difference between an adjustment
#   and an argument.
#
# Why it has its own table
#   Without one, the corpus's `between` rows would be read from the XML and dropped on every
#   ingest, and nothing would notice, because no rule reads them: a rule reads only what it needs,
#   so a field no rule reads is under no pressure to exist. Only asking whether a document can be
#   written back out finds a gap like that, and the query side cannot ask it.
#
# No foreign key on `notation`
#   `notation` is a reference to another document and may point at nothing, so it carries no
#   foreign key, as `composition/part_references.sqlc` carries none. `asrt:Elimination` refuses a
#   keyref that would restrict `between` to the fusion's own parts, because "a group eliminating
#   against a member that files nothing, or against a layer folded into a different composed
#   layer, is ordinary".
-- asrt:Fusion/asrt:eliminations/asrt:elimination/asrt:between, one row each.
SELECT b.composition, b.composed_layer, b.quantity, b.seq,
       b.party, b.notation, b.layer, b.version, b.regime,
       b.registration_taxonomy, b.registration_value
FROM pm.elimination_between b
